Title: The Night Shift
Credit: Written by
Author: Test Fixture

INT. HOSPITAL CORRIDOR - NIGHT

Fluorescent light. A gurney parked against the wall, empty. The
duty board still lists a patient who left four hours ago.

MARÍA
(low)
You signed the transfer at eleven and the bed was already cold
by then. I have the sheet in my pocket and your name is on it
twice, once at the top and once where it matters.

JOSÉ
I signed what they put in front of me, the way I have signed
every night for nine years, because the alternative is standing
here arguing while somebody downstairs waits.

MARÍA
Then read the next one before you sign it. That is all I am
asking and it costs you thirty seconds.

She walks. He does not follow.

INT. NURSES STATION - CONTINUOUS

A monitor cycles through empty beds. Someone is already typing
when the others arrive.

ZOË
Bed nine came back positive an hour ago and somebody has to
call the family before the morning shift finds out from a
chart. I am not doing it from this desk.

BJÖRN
Not tonight. Tonight we get the chart straight, and in the
morning, when there is a lawyer in the room and a record that
holds up, we tell them everything at once.

ZOË
The chart is straight. The person is not. You keep saying those
are the same sentence and they have never once been the same
sentence.

EXT. AMBULANCE BAY - LATER

Rain on the concrete. A paper cup sits untouched on the ledge
under the overhang.

RENÉE
I gave him the wrong bag. That is the whole of it, and every
minute I spend letting the three of you build a story around it
is a minute I spend lying.

MARÍA
Say that again in front of the board and you are finished here,
and finished anywhere that calls here to ask about you.

RENÉE
Then I am finished. I would rather be finished than spend
another year watching all of you round the corners off things
that do not have corners.

INT. HOSPITAL CORRIDOR - DAWN

The duty board is wiped clean. One name goes up on it and the
marker goes down beside it.

JOSÉ
(to himself)
Read the next one. Read the next one and then read the one
after that, and stop pretending the reading was ever the hard
part of any of this.

BJÖRN
You want me to sign it. You have wanted me to sign it since
midnight and you have been very patient about not saying so.

JOSÉ
I want you to read it first. If you still want to sign it
afterward then I will hold the pen for you.

INT. BOARD ROOM - MORNING

Six chairs, five people. The empty one gets taken anyway.

RENÉE
I am not going to make you ask. I know what the meeting is and
I know what the room decided before anybody sat down in it.

ZOË
Nobody was going to ask. That is the part you keep missing, and
it is the only part I would have warned you about.

MARÍA
That is the part that should worry you. A room that does not
ask is a room that already wrote the answer down somewhere.

EXT. PARKING STRUCTURE - DAY

A box goes into a car trunk. Then it comes back out, and the
walk back toward the building starts.

BJÖRN
(quietly)
Fine. I will read it. All of it, out loud, in front of whoever
is still sitting up there, and then we can find out together
what any of us is actually willing to sign.

FADE OUT.
